  "7_BODY_formula_checks": [
    {
      "step": 1,
      "title": "Derive the gradient bound internally",
      "text": "Finite dimension supplies completeness. Apply the existing quadratic-regularization Hessian theorem with r=0 to obtain LipschitzWith β (gradient V), hence continuity of the actual gradient. This closes a proof ingredient without adding a caller.",
      "formula": "\\|\\nabla V(x)-\\nabla V(u)\\|\\le\\beta\\|x-u\\|.",
      "source_region": {
        "start_line": 106,
        "end_line": 111,
        "RAW_start_inclusive": 5325,
        "RAW_end_exclusive": 5695,
        "exact_code_RAW_sha256": "6d8e01485130b776cfbf394a19b5c4f22ba5c4e748c5f71417aface0f5b84383",
        "exact_code_LF_sha256": "6d8e01485130b776cfbf394a19b5c4f22ba5c4e748c5f71417aface0f5b84383",
        "RAW_bytes": 370
      },
      "literal_BODY_exact": true,
      "source_review_status": "independent implementation/exposition preparation, final packet pending"
    },
    {
      "step": 2,
      "title": "Identify the zero-safe orthogonal reflection",
      "text": "Total division gives R(0,p)=p. For all n, R is the existing isometric reflection in the orthogonal complement of span(n). Its square is identity and it preserves norm. Expanding the inner product gives its sign reversal; the nonzero branch uses ‖n‖²≠0, while n=0 is handled separately.",
      "formula": "R_n=(\\operatorname{span}\\{n\\}^{\\perp})\\text{-reflection},\\quad R_n^2=I,\\quad\\|R_np\\|=\\|p\\|,\\quad\\langle R_np,n\\rangle=-\\langle p,n\\rangle.",
      "source_region": {
        "start_line": 112,
        "end_line": 130,
        "RAW_start_inclusive": 5695,
        "RAW_end_exclusive": 6559,
        "exact_code_RAW_sha256": "7d71f4da5285d52ce4f45190d3ed39a87c9c09ff300a15b61d8508f38c42a19c",
        "exact_code_LF_sha256": "7d71f4da5285d52ce4f45190d3ed39a87c9c09ff300a15b61d8508f38c42a19c",
        "RAW_bytes": 864
      },
      "literal_BODY_exact": true,
      "source_review_status": "independent implementation/exposition preparation, final packet pending"
    },
    {
      "step": 3,
      "title": "Check Borel and continuous operations",
      "text": "The state-dependent reflection uses continuous gradients, inner products and norms, together with total real division, which is Borel. Thus S is jointly Borel, including h=0. The positive-part rate uses no quotient, so it is jointly continuous and consequently Borel. Joint continuity of S is not required or asserted.",
      "formula": "S\\text{ is Borel},\\qquad\\lambda=\\sqrt\\eta\\max(0,\\langle p,h\\rangle)\\text{ is continuous}.",
      "source_region": {
        "start_line": 131,
        "end_line": 136,
        "RAW_start_inclusive": 6559,
        "RAW_end_exclusive": 6771,
        "exact_code_RAW_sha256": "7a0b8be7b0658694a9245858ab7420b596c14f92876477c4894167dca09a7a84",
        "exact_code_LF_sha256": "7a0b8be7b0658694a9245858ab7420b596c14f92876477c4894167dca09a7a84",
        "RAW_bytes": 212
      },
      "literal_BODY_exact": true,
      "source_review_status": "independent implementation/exposition preparation, final packet pending"
    },
    {
      "step": 4,
      "title": "Prove the actual bounce and rate identities",
      "text": "The position stays fixed, so its residual h stays fixed. Reflection involution proves S²=I and norm preservation proves exact H conservation. Its pairing sign reversal gives the flipped rate. For a=⟨p,h⟩, the two sign cases prove max(0,a)−max(0,−a)=a. The zero-residual case gives identity and zero rate.",
      "formula": "S^2=I,\\quad H(Sz)=H(z),\\quad\\lambda(Sz)=\\sqrt\\eta\\max(0,-a),\\quad\\lambda(z)-\\lambda(Sz)=\\sqrt\\eta a.",
      "source_region": {
        "start_line": 137,
        "end_line": 161,
        "RAW_start_inclusive": 6771,
        "RAW_end_exclusive": 7972,
        "exact_code_RAW_sha256": "aaaec4dece4b294e347a4b087f0fc150745e28c923b82a365fde95301a5a2daf",
        "exact_code_LF_sha256": "aaaec4dece4b294e347a4b087f0fc150745e28c923b82a365fde95301a5a2daf",
        "RAW_bytes": 1201
      },
      "literal_BODY_exact": true,
      "source_review_status": "independent implementation/exposition preparation, final packet pending"
    },
    {
      "step": 5,
      "title": "Keep zero energy and collect the first nine laws",
      "text": "η>0 makes η⁻¹ nonnegative, so H is nonnegative as the weighted sum of two squared vector norms divided by two. Collect the first nine laws, then fix arbitrary y,xRef,z₀ and z on its actual energy layer.",
      "formula": "\\mathcal E=H(y,x_{\\rm ref},z_0)\\ge0.",
      "source_region": {
        "start_line": 162,
        "end_line": 169,
        "RAW_start_inclusive": 7972,
        "RAW_end_exclusive": 8347,
        "exact_code_RAW_sha256": "8812537fb901c83a0c24f2f1f1003c0c95528dbb65b6921bd665aab95187eef1",
        "exact_code_LF_sha256": "8812537fb901c83a0c24f2f1f1003c0c95528dbb65b6921bd665aab95187eef1",
        "RAW_bytes": 375
      },
      "literal_BODY_exact": true,
      "source_review_status": "independent implementation/exposition preparation, final packet pending"
    },
    {
      "step": 6,
      "title": "Extract the two energy-layer radii",
      "text": "The equality H(z)=ℰ gives η⁻¹‖x−c‖²+‖p‖²=2ℰ. Dropping each nonnegative summand gives the momentum radius and, after multiplication by positive η, the position radius. Taking square roots requires no ℰ>0 and no division by ℰ.",
      "formula": "\\|p\\|^2\\le2\\mathcal E,\\quad\\|x-c\\|^2\\le2\\eta\\mathcal E,\\quad\\|p\\|\\le\\sqrt{2\\mathcal E},\\quad\\|x-c\\|\\le\\sqrt{2\\eta\\mathcal E}.",
      "source_region": {
        "start_line": 170,
        "end_line": 187,
        "RAW_start_inclusive": 8347,
        "RAW_end_exclusive": 9351,
        "exact_code_RAW_sha256": "e79dcea715c69802df39c95056052be9a37ed8b9c31d0779380a6e54ae9648b7",
        "exact_code_LF_sha256": "e79dcea715c69802df39c95056052be9a37ed8b9c31d0779380a6e54ae9648b7",
        "RAW_bytes": 1004
      },
      "literal_BODY_exact": true,
      "source_review_status": "independent implementation/exposition preparation, final packet pending"
    },
    {
      "step": 7,
      "title": "Derive the exact pointwise majorant",
      "text": "The triangle inequality bounds ‖x−xRef‖ by ‖x−c‖+‖c−xRef‖. Apply the internally derived β-Lipschitz estimate to h, then Cauchy–Schwarz and max(0,⟨p,h⟩)≤‖p‖‖h‖. Insert both radii and multiply by √η≥0. The resulting Λ is the printed same-energy-layer majorant, not a stochastic path or cost bound.",
      "formula": "\\lambda(z)\\le\\sqrt\\eta\\|p\\|\\|h\\|\\le\\sqrt\\eta\\,\\beta\\sqrt{2\\mathcal E}\\bigl(\\sqrt{2\\eta\\mathcal E}+\\|c-x_{\\rm ref}\\|\\bigr).",
      "source_region": {
        "start_line": 188,
        "end_line": 208,
        "RAW_start_inclusive": 9351,
        "RAW_end_exclusive": 10495,
        "exact_code_RAW_sha256": "61d3566cf98137637c988d0ea605c00d5d9dd15d96a11de932d59bf8b83b0f82",
        "exact_code_LF_sha256": "61d3566cf98137637c988d0ea605c00d5d9dd15d96a11de932d59bf8b83b0f82",
        "RAW_bytes": 1144
      },
